{% extends 'shells/docs.html.twig' %}
{% block title %}Middleware · php-via Docs{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
<article>
    <h1>Middleware</h1>
    <p>
        php-via runs <strong>PSR-15 middleware</strong> for cross-cutting concerns such as authentication,
        CORS, logging and rate limiting. Middleware runs on page loads, actions and plain routes; implement
        <code>SseAwareMiddleware</code> to run on SSE connects too.
    </p>

    <h2 id="how-it-works">How it works</h2>
    <p>
        Middleware follows the <a href="https://www.php-fig.org/psr/psr-15/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PSR-15</a>
        onion model. Each middleware gets a PSR-7 <code>ServerRequestInterface</code> and either answers
        itself or passes the request on with <code>$handler-&gt;handle()</code>. php-via converts the
        OpenSwoole request to PSR-7 only when middleware is registered, so an app without middleware pays
        nothing.
    </p>
    <p>
        On a page load and an action, php-via writes the response itself once the request reaches it. A
        response the middleware returns without calling <code>$handler-&gt;handle()</code>, such as a redirect
        to a login page, goes out as it is. The response that <code>$handler-&gt;handle()</code> returns is a
        placeholder: headers a middleware adds to it do not reach the browser. Set cookies with
        <code>$c-&gt;setCookie()</code>, and see <a href="/docs/twig#csp">Content-Security-Policy</a> for a
        policy with a nonce. On a <a href="#plain-routes">plain route</a> the handler's response goes out as
        it is, with what middleware added.
    </p>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Request</th><th>Global middleware</th><th>Route middleware</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Page load</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Rebuilding a tab that was away (revival)</td><td>as for the action or connect that rebuilds it</td><td>yes, on a GET of the page's URL</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Action</td><td>yes</td><td>no</td></tr>
            <tr><td>SSE connect</td><td><code>SseAwareMiddleware</code> only</td><td>no</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>route()</code></td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 id="global-middleware">Global middleware</h2>
    <p>
        Register global middleware with <code>$app-&gt;middleware()</code> before <code>$app-&gt;start()</code>:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
use Tuupola\Middleware\CorsMiddleware;

$app->middleware(new CorsMiddleware([
    'origin' => ['https://example.com'],
    'methods' => ['GET', 'POST'],
    'headers.allow' => ['Content-Type', 'Authorization'],
    'credentials' => true,
]));
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h2 id="route-middleware">Per-route middleware</h2>
    <p>
        Attach middleware to one route with the <code>RouteDefinition</code> that <code>page()</code>,
        <code>mount()</code> and <code>route()</code> return, or to several with <code>group()</code>:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->page('/admin', fn (Context $c) => $c->view('admin.html.twig'))
    ->middleware(new AuthMiddleware($app));

$app->page('/reports', fn (Context $c) => $c->view('reports.html.twig'))
    ->middleware(new AuthMiddleware($app), new AuditMiddleware());

$app->group('/account', function (Via $app): void {
    $app->page('/', fn (Context $c) => $c->view('account.html.twig'));
    $app->page('/billing', fn (Context $c) => $c->view('billing.html.twig'));
})->middleware(new AuthMiddleware($app));
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        Route middleware runs after the global middleware: global, then route, then the page handler.
    </p>

    <h2 id="writing-middleware">Writing middleware</h2>
    <p>
        Implement <code>Psr\Http\Server\MiddlewareInterface</code>. Every request carries the visitor's session
        id in the <code>via.session</code> attribute, the id <code>$c-&gt;getSessionId()</code> returns, so
        middleware never reads the session cookie, whose name <code>withSecureCookie()</code> changes. A request
        without the cookie gets the id of the session the page then starts. Pass data on with
        <code>$request-&gt;withAttribute()</code>: the page handler reads it with
        <code>$c-&gt;getRequestAttribute()</code>.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
use Mbolli\PhpVia\Via;
use Nyholm\Psr7\Response;
use Psr\Http\Message\ResponseInterface;
use Psr\Http\Message\ServerRequestInterface;
use Psr\Http\Server\MiddlewareInterface;
use Psr\Http\Server\RequestHandlerInterface;

final class AuthMiddleware implements MiddlewareInterface {
    public function __construct(private Via $app) {}

    public function process(ServerRequestInterface $request, RequestHandlerInterface $handler): ResponseInterface {
        $user = $this->app->getSessionData($request->getAttribute('via.session'), 'user');
        if ($user === null) {
            return new Response(302, ['Location' => '/login']);
        }

        // Available in the page handler as $c->getRequestAttribute('user')
        return $handler->handle($request->withAttribute('user', $user));
    }
}
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <div class="callout callout-warning">
        <strong>Middleware lives as long as the worker.</strong> One instance serves every request in the
        worker process, so never keep per-request state on its properties. Pass data on with
        <code>$request-&gt;withAttribute()</code>.
    </div>

    <h2 id="sse-middleware">SSE-aware middleware</h2>
    <p>
        Global middleware skips the SSE connect unless it implements the <code>SseAwareMiddleware</code>
        marker interface. Implement it when the stream needs the same gate as the page, such as an auth check:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
use Mbolli\PhpVia\Http\Middleware\SseAwareMiddleware;

final class AuthMiddleware implements SseAwareMiddleware {
    public function process(ServerRequestInterface $request, RequestHandlerInterface $handler): ResponseInterface {
        // runs on page loads, actions and SSE connects
        return $handler->handle($request);
    }
}

$app->middleware(new AuthMiddleware());
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        The marker counts only for global middleware. Route middleware never runs on an SSE connect, so a
        tab that was open before a logout keeps its stream until it reloads or its context expires.
    </p>

    <h2 id="request-attributes">Request attributes in handlers and actions</h2>
    <p>
        The attributes middleware sets reach the <code>Context</code>:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->page('/dashboard', function (Context $c): void {
    $user = $c->getRequestAttribute('user');

    $c->view('dashboard.html.twig', ['name' => $user['name']]);
})->middleware(new AuthMiddleware($app));
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        In an action, <code>getRequestAttribute()</code> returns what the global middleware set on that action's
        request; each action has its own, also when two run at once. Route middleware does not run on actions,
        so an attribute it sets is there in the page handler only: read it there and pass it into the action's
        closure, or register the middleware globally. Outside a request, such as in a timer, the attributes are
        those of the page request.
    </p>
    <p id="revival">
        When a tab comes back after its context was destroyed, php-via rebuilds the context and runs the page
        handler again behind the route's middleware, on a GET of the page's URL with the cookies of the request
        that rebuilds it. An auth gate therefore applies again, and its attributes reach the handler. When the
        middleware answers itself, such as with a redirect to the login page, the tab reloads and the page load
        gets the middleware's answer. See <a href="/docs/lifecycle#revival">Lifecycle → Revival</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="plain-routes">Plain routes</h2>
    <p>
        <code>$app-&gt;route()</code> serves a PSR-15 handler with no context, shell or template, for JSON,
        webhooks or an MCP server, behind the global and the route's middleware. The request carries the path
        parameters and <code>via.session</code> as attributes, and the handler's response goes out with what the
        middleware added. php-via checks no <code>Origin</code> header on these routes, so add CSRF or auth
        middleware where a route changes state.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->route(['GET', 'OPTIONS'], '/api/items/{id}', new ItemHandler())
    ->middleware(new ApiKeyMiddleware());
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>See <a href="/docs/api#via-route"><code>route()</code></a> for methods, HEAD and uploads.</p>

    <h2 id="example">Live example: auth middleware</h2>
    <p>
        The <a href="/examples/login">Login Flow example</a> protects <code>/examples/login/dashboard</code>
        with an <code>AuthMiddleware</code>. Visitors who are not logged in go to the login form; logged-in
        visitors see a dashboard with their session data. Its login and logout call
        <code>regenerateSession()</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="security">Built-in security</h2>
    <p>
        php-via has these protections without any middleware:
    </p>

    <h3 id="csrf">CSRF protection</h3>
    <p>
        Actions check the <code>Origin</code> header. Configure the allowed origins with
        <code>withTrustedOrigins()</code>:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config = (new Config())
    ->withTrustedOrigins(['https://myapp.com', 'https://staging.myapp.com']);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <ul>
        <li><code>null</code> (default): the origin's host must match the <code>Host</code> header</li>
        <li><code>[]</code>: refuse every request that carries an origin</li>
        <li><code>['https://example.com']</code>: only these origins</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Outside dev mode an action without an <code>Origin</code> header gets 403 in every case, unless
        <code>withAllowMissingOrigin()</code> lets non-browser clients through. Plain routes are not checked.
    </p>

    <h3 id="session-cookies">Session cookies</h3>
    <p>
        php-via accepts only session ids in the form it issues, 32 lowercase hex characters; any other cookie
        value starts a new session. Turn on <code>withSecureCookie()</code> for a site served over HTTPS: the
        cookie gets the <code>__Host-</code> prefix, which requires HTTPS, <code>Path=/</code> and no
        <code>Domain</code>, and the plain cookie is then ignored.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config = (new Config())
    ->withSecureCookie(true);       // __Host-via_session_id; Secure; SameSite=Lax
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h3 id="session-rotation">Session rotation</h3>
    <p>
        A login should give the session a new cookie, so a cookie someone planted or read before it no longer
        reaches the account. In an action, call <code>$c-&gt;regenerateSession()</code>; in middleware or a
        <code>route()</code> handler, <code>$app-&gt;regenerateSession($request)</code>, before
        <code>$handler-&gt;handle()</code>. The session keeps its id, so <code>via.session</code>, session data
        and the session's tabs stay as they are. The session rotates at the call: the old cookie works for 10
        more seconds, and then the streams opened with it end. Call it at logout too, with
        <code>clearSessionData()</code>. See <a href="/docs/api#context-regenerate-session">regenerateSession()</a>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="rate-limiting">Action rate limiting</h3>
    <p>
        A per-IP sliding-window limit for actions, which answers 429 past it:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config = (new Config())
    ->withActionRateLimit(100, 60);  // at most 100 actions per 60 seconds per IP
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

